-- Version 16 to 17: give the seeded service templates an identity every device agrees on.
--
-- `seedDefaultsIfEmpty` runs once per device and generated an id for each of the four
-- defaults, so two devices produced two full sets of the same templates. Harmless while
-- service_template did not synchronise; the moment it does, a studio sees everything twice.
--
-- The id becomes the studio plus the template's name — the same value
-- `defaultTemplateId` produces, which is why that function builds a readable string rather
-- than a hash: this statement has to reproduce it in SQL.
--
-- Matched on name, so a template the studio has renamed keeps the id it already had. That
-- is deliberate: it has stopped being one of ours and become one of theirs, and giving it
-- a derived id would merge it with another device's untouched copy.
--
-- No schema change. Rows are rewritten in place.
--
-- These statements must produce exactly the schema the .sq files describe; the
-- verifyMigrations task compares the two and fails the build if they diverge.

UPDATE service_template
SET id = studio_id || ':default:' || name
WHERE name IN (
    'Wedding — Full Day',
    'Brand Video — Single Day',
    'Real Estate — Standard Listing',
    'Headshots — Personal Branding'
)
  AND id != studio_id || ':default:' || name;
